we're about to travel through a mouse's  leg bone  this tiny canal normally carries blood  into or out of the bone marrow  researchers have known for a long time  that bones have an efficient system of  blood vessels to enable immune cells  made here in the bone marrow to move  quickly into general circulation but  they've only just managed to see the  many microscopic vessels that play a key  role in that process as we zoom out you  can see lots of small holes marking the  entrance and exit points of these teeny  capillaries without knowing it trauma  surgeons have been making use of this  fast and direct transport system for  years when they can't get quick access  to a patient's veins instead they inject  drugs or fluid directly into the  patient's leg bone this technique first  developed by the military and used on  battlefields is called intraosseous  infusion to figure out how intraosseous  infusions work scientists turned to a  technique called clearing this involves  washing away the fatty membranes that  surround cells to make whole organs  see-through it's been applied to many  structures including hearts making bones  see-through has proved to trickier but  recently a team in Germany managed it  this mouse bone the size of a matchstick  is a tibia or leg bone the team found  that by using a chemical called ethyl  cinemate a component of cinnamon they  could make the opaque bone become like  glass leaving the blood vessels intact  and visible now using a microscope the  researchers could examine the blood  vessels they could clearly see the large  arteries and veins that are known to  carry blood in and out of the bone  marrow but looking more closely they  also noticed hundreds of tiny blood  vessels running straight through the  bone from the bone marrow to the outside  the team named them transcortical  vessels or TC V's what's more by  staining a kind of immune cell called a  neutrophil the team was able to watch  immune cells the yellow blobs actually  traveling through the T series these  long overlooked vessels seem to play a  really important role in bony physiology  in fact the team calculated that the  majority of blood flowing in and out of  bones actually passes through them and  these vessels are not unique to long leg  bones  a few months ago another team described  similar microscopic channels that cross  the skull and connect directly with the  surface of the brain this rather blurry  video also shows immune cells moving  through the channels understanding how  immune cells move from the bone marrow  to local sites of injury or disease  could help us to treat stroke as well as  inflammatory bone conditions such as  arthritis so far the experiments have  been done in mouse models of these  conditions but the team's suspect that T  Seavey's are common in human bones too  to look for them one of the authors did  an MRI scan of his own leg this is a  cross-section of his thigh if we focus  on the bone and then zoom in you can see  tiny holes that look like the openings  to the tiny vessels seen in mice signs  of a network of previously unrecognized  blood channels that open up new routes  for medical research  you
 

